codex
Set up a proof harness for `_receive` with realistic packet modeling and nondet branches to exercise both success and error paths, then wired a CBMC Makefile and documented the required stubs. The harness in `_receive_harness.c` includes `gnrc_tcp_eventloop.c`, builds a 3‑snip packet chain with TCP/IPv6 headers, and uses minimal stubs for missing GNRC/TCP helpers while keeping behavior aligned with the reported “no-body” errors. The Makefile in `_receive/Makefile` defines the needed module macros and `MAXTHREADS=1` to keep mutex handling safe and deterministic. Stub provenance is recorded in `_receive/stubs.log`.

CBMC results: `build/reports/cbmc.xml` reports `SUCCESS`, and `build/report/json/viewer-coverage.json` shows all `_receive` lines hit/both with no missed lines in `sys/net/gnrc/transport_layer/tcp/gnrc_tcp_eventloop.c`.

Commands run:
- `make clean && make`
- `make report`

Files:
- `_receive/_receive_harness.c`
- `_receive/Makefile`
- `_receive/stubs.log`

If you want, I can:
1) Open the HTML coverage report at `cbmc/codex_tests/_receive/build/report/html/viewer-coverage.html`.
2) Tighten or expand any harness assumptions to focus on specific TCP behaviors.
